      "inputTokens": 279,
      "outputTokens": 2036,
      "costUsd": 0.0184635,
      "pronunciationText": "The last kind works backwards. A coaster track follows y equals x cubed, plus ay x squared, plus b x, and its dip is a stationary point at one, minus two. Find a and b. A stationary point tells you two things, so it gives you two equations. The point is on the curve, so f of one equals minus two, which gives a plus b equals minus three. And the curve is flat there, so f prime of one equals zero, which gives two a plus b equals minus three. Subtract the equations, and ay is zero, so b is minus three. The curve is x cubed minus three x, the coaster track from the global maximum video. Its derivative is also zero at x equals minus one. The gradient goes from positive to negative there, so minus one, two is a local maximum. Using only one of the two facts is the classic slip, because that one equation leaves a whole family of possible a and b."
    },
